Ñòiª¶ç#âþ¿FäúëÎ.?"—«kÚl_'ëX9A{"rendered":{"html":"<p>An attribute goes before a function and says how to run it, for example\nwhether a build step may be cached or how long it may take. GCSL checks\nevery attribute. Most are read by the tool that runs the program, such as\na build system, and GCSL itself acts on its own two, <code>retry</code> and\n<code>timeout</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true}\n\n#[hermetic]\nfn compile(source: String) -&gt; String { \"compiled {source}\" }\n\n#[hermetic(false)] #[timeout(10m)]\nfn deploy(target: String) -&gt; String { \"deployed {target}\" }\n\nresult = deploy(compile(\"main.c\"))\n</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"attributes--declaring-an-attribute\">Declaring an attribute <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#attributes--declaring-an-attribute\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>An attribute's arguments form a record, so you declare an attribute as a\nrecord type marked <code>#[attribute]</code>. Each field is an argument, and a field\nwith a default is an argument you can leave out:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[attribute] type device = {name: String, count: Int = 1}\n\n#[device(name = \"kvm\")]\nfn boot() -&gt; String { \"booted\" }\n\nbooted = boot()\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Arguments must be constants: literals, or lists and records of them. A\nfield may be an <code>Int</code>, <code>Float</code>, <code>Bool</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Duration</code> or <code>Size</code>, or\na list or record of those. An attribute is declared, imported and named in\nthe same way as any other type, so a tool usually provides its attributes\nin a module that you import.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attributes--using-an-attribute\">Using an attribute <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#attributes--using-an-attribute\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Write <code>#[NAME]</code>, or <code>#[NAME(ARGUMENTS)]</code>, on the line before a function: a\n<code>fn</code>, a <code>def</code>, or a definition made with a definition type. An attribute\nwith one field can take its argument by position, as in <code>#[timeout(10m)]</code>;\none with more fields takes its arguments by name. A misspelt attribute is\nan error, so a mistake isn't silently ignored.</p>\n<p>GCSL provides two attributes of its own, <code>retry</code> and <code>timeout</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Argument</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>#[retry(times)]</code></td><td><code>times: Int</code></td><td>If the call fails, run it again, up to this many more times.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>#[timeout(limit)]</code></td><td><code>limit: Duration</code></td><td>Fail the call if it runs for longer than this.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p>A retried call runs its whole body again with the same arguments, so any\neffects it has happen again: use <code>retry</code> only where that's safe. Retrying a\npure function can't change its result, so GCSL warns about it. When a call\ntimes out, a program it's running is stopped. A function's own timeout\ncounts as a failure that its <code>retry</code> can retry, and each attempt gets the\nfull time. A timeout of an enclosing call is never retried by a call inside\nit.</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[retry(2)] #[timeout(30s)]\nfn fetch(url: String) -&gt; String { exec([\"echo\", \"fetched {url}\"]).stdout }\n\npage = fetch(\"https://example.com\")\n</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"attributes--attributes-on-a-definition-type\">Attributes on a definition type <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#attributes--attributes-on-a-definition-type\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Attributes on a definition type apply to every definition made with it.\nA definition's own attribute of the same name replaces the definition\ntype's:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true}\n\n#[def_type] #[hermetic]\ndef check(name: String, f: fn(): Bool): fn(): Bool { f }\n\ndef check fast() { true }\n#[hermetic(false)] def check online() { true }\n\nresults = [(check fast)()(), (check online)()()]\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here <code>fast</code> is hermetic and <code>online</code> is not.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"attributes--declaring-an-attribute","title":"Declaring an attribute","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"attributes--using-an-attribute","title":"Using an attribute","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"attributes--attributes-on-a-definition-type","title":"Attributes on a definition type","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}